// #110 — THE COUNTING ORACLE. `struct WO { o: Option<Inner> }` is moved once;
// `Inner`'s destructor must run EXACTLY ONCE.
//
// Before the fix it ran TWICE. compute_auto_copy_types' `is_copy_field` enum
// arm (sema.cpp) asked `TypeRef(t).struct_name()` — EMPTY on an Enum TypeRef,
// which carries `enum_name()` — against the BARE `enums_` key, while `enums_`
// is only ever written qualified (`enums_[sema_key(pkg,name)]`). Two independent
// misses on one lookup, both landing on the generous `return true`, so the
// payload arm below them had ZERO executions and EVERY enum-typed field read as
// Copy. `WO` was therefore auto-promoted into `copy_types_`, `is_move_type(WO)`
// answered false, and NO move was ever tracked for it — while
// `has_droppable_fields` (which answers the enum question correctly) still put
// drop glue on every slot the value occupied. Copy and droppable at once: one
// value, one destructor call PER SLOT. With a heap-owning field, a double free.
//
// ⚠ BOTH DIRECTIONS. Two lines is the pre-fix miscompile; ZERO lines would be a
// suppressed destructor — a leak — which is how the opposite defect is born
// (#103 closed exactly that symptom). Neither may be accepted as "fixed".
package dropenumfield;
use logos.std.fmt;
struct Inner { pub n: i64 }
impl Drop for Inner { fn drop(self: &mut Inner) { println!("DROP n={}", self.n); } }
struct WO { pub o: Option<Inner> }
fn main() -> i32 {
    let b: WO = WO { o: Option::Some(Inner { n: 7i64 }) };
    let c: WO = b;
    println!("moved");
    return 0i32;
}
